      "writtenOn": "2021-09-16T09:26:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ping?\n\nSome platforms, such as those that are still using OpenSSL 1.0 will fail to verify the default chain that Let\u0027s Encrypt is returning after September 29th 2021. As such, the alternate certificate chain is necessary and it would be good if this could land ASAP (otherwise we\u0027ll need to fork golang.org/x/crypto).",
      "revId": "2cd8e17330f6e964591873a6a432056c69588b32",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "73ad35f5_a344e5a0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 17920
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-16T13:57:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually, after reading [1], it seems like nothing will break after September 29th. The longer cross-signed chain will still be returned as the default. This is still necessary if you don\u0027t need to support older Android devices and want the shorter chain but the default chain isn\u0027t changing any time soon.\n\n[1] https://community.letsencrypt.org/t/production-chain-changes/150739",
      "parentUuid": "f564aa0b_f1677652",
      "revId": "2cd8e17330f6e964591873a6a432056c69588b32",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b29325cb_ac41ad61",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 13640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-16T15:21:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The DST Root CA X3 expires on the 30th of September 2021 (as noted in the referenced document) - some things (e.g. Android) ignore the not after date for trusted certificates and will continue to verify to DST Root CA X3, while many others will verify to the ISRG Root X1 (ignoring the DST Root CA X3 based intermediate). \n\nHowever, some older (broken) platforms will fail to verify with the default certificate chain, as they build a chain up to the DST Root CA X3 using the supplied intermediates, before checking the not after date and failing hard (rather than considering alternate chains):\n\n$ openssl version\nOpenSSL 1.0.1f\n$ echo Q | openssl s_client -CApath /etc/ssl/certs -connect community.letsencrypt.org:443 | grep \u0027Verify return code\u0027\n    Verify return code: 0 (ok)\n$ echo Q | faketime 2021-10-02 openssl s_client -CApath /etc/ssl/certs -connect community.letsencrypt.org:443 | grep \u0027Verify return code\u0027\n    Verify return code: 10 (certificate has expired)\n\nThat said, I think the commit message update is fine.",
      "parentUuid": "73ad35f5_a344e5a0",
      "revId": "2cd8e17330f6e964591873a6a432056c69588b32",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705"
    },
